 note that the  entire community put that chair there  and it wasn't just concerned parents  that man that chair because irrespective  of whether we are or whether we intend  to be parents or not we will all belong  in communities where there will be kids  and this is why the issue of at-risk  youth is relevant to all of us now I  don't want this talk to be all doom and  gloom for 12 minutes so I want to also  share that the good news is there are  things both big and small that we all  can do actually that you can do to  really change a child's life I want to  share the story of a young boy this boy  grew up with his mother and when he  turned 9 his mother thought it'd be a  good idea for him to spend some time  with his dad who was living overseas now  what this mother and the boy did not  know was that the environment that this  boy was to hidden to with his dad and  with his dad's new wife that it would be  an abuse of one so imagine the situation  we've got a young boy in a new country  he doesn't speak a word of the local  language he's in an abusive environment  with no friends or family to help him  out what's a boy to do he does what he  knows to do which is run away from it  all he runs away from home obviously  doesn't go to school anymore and even  has to steal food in order to survive  cops would catch this boy stealing he'd  be returned back to his family and the  whole vicious cycle would continue that  is until one day a shopkeeper catches  this boy stealing and unlike everyone  else before him the shopkeeper decided  not to call the cops but to call his  friend who also happened to be a social  worker now to cut a very long story  short there is a happy ending this one  phone call meant that this boy was able  to get into a stable home environment  focus on his education and ultimately  become amongst the first in his family  to graduate from college now I share the  story to illustrate the power of one  little thing in being able to change a  child's life in this story it was that  one phone call that started to change  this boy's life now I know about the  power of a little thing in being able to  take a child off the streets to be  standing here in front of you today  because that boy was me and that story  is mine and my life was changed because  of one of these little things now I  acknowledge that this is a big tricky  issue it's a tough problem to solve and  has hooks and societal issues such as  poverty and I also recognize that you  never quite know when these little  things will truly be able to change a  child's life again back to the good news  the good news is there are tons of great  organizations that try and solve this  problem in a big in a far more  systematic way and I just like to  highlight a couple today that I've had  the fortune I've been involved with so  the first is project K so they are a  youth mentoring program based back home  in New Zealand actually very similar to  Big Brothers Big Sisters here in the US  which I'm  many of you are familiar with one hope  United is based in Chicago and when I  was working there I was fortunate enough  to be involved with them to help develop  a program that provides services and  resources to low-income families so they  can provide a stable home environment  for their kids and in fact right now i'm  a board fellow with a great organization  based right here in palo alto name  peninsula bridge now for those of you  that don't know board fellows meant to  help the bridge but meant to help the  organization that they're worth but i  think i create more homework for some my  board members than i do and helping them  but bridge provides great summer  programs are over for for low income for  low resource students so they can be  prepared to graduate from high school  now my own organization isn't quite  ready to be Placid up here well if we  had a screen but I've started working  towards my own organization that looks  after kids not only on these 